IMPORT `il47-nouns`

-- Row il-47, first part. THE CITATIONS OF "SCHEDULE B" IN FOUR REGULATIONS, as typed values.
-- A citation is encoded as TWO descriptions: what the citing words say they point at, and what the target is. A test compares them. Where they differ, the citation does not point at what it says;
-- that is a defect, kept in an expected-red file with its count declared in check.sh and in encoding.json.
-- Eight citations are recorded. Six are defects: four regulations, made between 1971 and 1979 (before the Law of 1995), cite "לוח ב׳ לחוק" (Schedule B of the Law); fork R1 in NOTES.md asks which lettering of the schedules those words use.
-- In the Law as enacted in 1995 (Sefer HaChukim 1522, p. 299) Schedule B is the Treasury allocation schedule, which has items 1 and 2 only; the schedule the regulations are read to mean (fork R1) is printed on p. 301 as Schedule ט, marked [ב׳].
-- Schedule B was repealed by Sefer HaChukim 2511 (30 November 2015), p. 248, item (4), commencing 1 January 2016 (p. 249, s 31).
-- Two are sound and are here as controls: regulations that cite "לוח ט׳ לחוק", the lettering of the Law of 1995.
-- LIMBS. Limb (a) is the question whether the target is as the citing words say; limb (b) is the question whether the citing words still read so in the latest official text of the citing provision.
-- Each limb says where it was read from: the official instrument (a page image), the consolidation and the latest amending instrument, or the consolidation only (the Hebrew Wikisource text, which is not official).
-- TWO READINGS (fork R1 in NOTES.md). The literal reading takes the letter as the Law of 1995 uses it; the other reading takes the old letter as the schedule that carried it before 1995, which is how I read the letters printed in
-- square brackets on the page ([ב׳] beside Schedule ט on p. 301, [י״ד] beside Schedule B on p. 299); no page read says what the brackets mean. The field `the target if the old lettering is read as the new` holds that second reading.
-- A date for which the sources held give no answer is NOTHING; there is no REFUSE.

§ `Cross-references`

GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the citation points at what it says` MEANS c's `the citing words say it points at` EQUALS c's `the target is`

GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the citation points at what it says if the old lettering is read as the new` MEANS c's `the citing words say it points at` EQUALS c's `the target if the old lettering is read as the new`

GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`both limbs were read from the official instruments` MEANS
    c's `limb (a) was read from` EQUALS `the official instrument, read from its page image`
    AND c's `limb (b) was read from` EQUALS `the official instrument, read from its page image`

-- Whether the target stands in the official text on a day. The only answer the sources held give is the day it was repealed and the days from then on; for any earlier day, and for a target
-- with no recorded repeal, the answer is NOTHING (no source held says when the target was added, or that it was never repealed).
GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
      day IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the target stands in the official text on` MEANS
    CONSIDER c's `the target was repealed from`
        WHEN JUST gone THEN IF day AT LEAST gone THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

